    <string name="settings_hdr_strength">HDR strength</string>
    <string name="settings_hdr_strength_body" formatted="false">How much of the camera\'s HDR counts. 100% is the full HDR, 0% is the camera\'s own SDR version. You can set it per photo too.</string>
    <string name="settings_keep_hdr">Keep HDR</string>
    <string name="settings_keep_hdr_sub">The photo stays an HDR photo (Ultra HDR) and only gets smaller. On an HDR screen you see the extra brightness, apps that do not know HDR show the normal version. Off: an SDR photo made with the tone map below.</string>
    <string name="settings_hide_originals">Hide originals</string>
    <string name="settings_hide_originals_sub">When the conversion worked and the output checks out, the original moves to the trash of your Gallery: no more duplicate photos in your feed. Only JPEGs from DCIM, never RAWs, Lightroom exports or edits. Android removes it itself after the trash keeping period (usually 30 days); until then you can restore it.</string>
    <string name="settings_hide_originals_warn_title">Note</string>
    <string name="settings_hide_originals_warn">Without Keep HDR, the original\'s HDR is gone once it leaves the trash: Immich only backs up the Keeper folder. And while the original is in the trash, you cannot convert that photo again in Keeper or give it another HDR strength; restore it in the Gallery first.</string>
    <string name="settings_manage_media">Hide without asking</string>
    <string name="settings_manage_media_sub">Give Keeper media management access and Android does not have to ask every time. You turn that on yourself in the system settings.</string>
